# Build artifacts
native/carplay-jni/target/
netprobe_app/app/build/
netprobe_app/build/
netprobe_app/.gradle/
**/*.class

# OS
.DS_Store
**/.DS_Store

# Tooling scratch
.serena/

# Raw iPhone os_log captures: hundreds of MB each, and 99.9% of it is unrelated system chatter.
# tri_capture.sh already writes a filtered `iphone_carplay.log` beside them; distil that further into
# a `*_key.log` and commit THAT. Never commit the raw stream.
evidence/**/iphone_syslog.log
evidence/**/iphone_carplay.log

# Session scratch. These are LARGE (a single drive capture ran to 3.1 GB, logs/ to 338 MB) and are
# regenerated by tooling, so they are working state rather than results. Ignored explicitly because
# a `git add -A` swept them into a commit on 2026-08-28 — 3.4M insertions, .git 43 MB -> 438 MB, and
# an amend + reflog expire + gc to get back. The rule this encodes: anything a capture script writes
# is ignored by default, and evidence worth keeping is promoted into evidence/ deliberately.
logs/
.drive_capture_dir

# NOT audit/ — deliberately. `audit/CONSOLIDATED_2026-08-27.md` is hand-synthesised, adversarially
# reviewed, and cited by 11_HARDENING_PLAN.md as the SOLE authority for its whole R6 section
# ("the review wins ... the only text cleared for planning"). Ignoring it made ~30% of doc 11 rest
# on a file that would not exist on a fresh clone. It was swept in on 2026-08-29 by a scratch rule
# written for tool-regenerated output, which this is not. If a genuinely regenerated audit lands
# here later, ignore that file by name — not the directory.

# Dirty builds are reproducible from their sha and are not rollback targets — only the sha-clean
# stamps and the golden v*.apk are worth keeping.
apk/*-dirty.apk

# Keep: apk/ (golden rollback artifacts), evidence/, docs/, reference/, tools/, source

# Convenience symlink rewritten by every build — tracking it made every build dirty the
# tree, which made build_apk.sh stamp -dirty on clean checkouts and rendered the flag useless.
apk/netprobe-debug-latest.apk

# Bench instrument build output. ocbm-probe is built out-of-tree (CARGO_TARGET_DIR=/tmp) so it
# never dirties the APK sha stamp, but guard against an in-tree build anyway.
tools/ocbm-probe/target/

# Build output and capture scratch, now that this project lives under ccpa_custom/host. The
# sha-stamped APKs and the golden rollback build are NOT carried here — they remain in the
# standalone gm_ccpa checkout, together with the evidence corpus.
#
# NO TRAILING SLASH, deliberately. `apk/` matches a directory only, and apk/ is a SYMLINK into
# ~/.cache (see below) — the pattern stopped matching the moment it was relinked, and git offered the
# symlink for commit. A committed symlink to an absolute ~/.cache path is worse than a committed
# build: it resolves to nothing on any other machine. Slashless matches both shapes.
apk
evidence
logs

# Build artifacts and caches must never live under ~/Documents on this Mac: it is iCloud
# "Desktop & Documents" synced, and iCloud drops conflict copies (`classes 2.jar`, `Foo$Bar 2.dex`)
# into build trees, which the toolchain then eats as real inputs — nondeterministic duplicate-class
# failures that survive a clean until sync catches up. So:
#   * cargo output  -> $CARGO_TARGET_DIR, set by tools/build_apk.sh to ~/.cache/cargo-targets/
#   * APK output    -> apk/ is a symlink to ~/.cache/carlink-artifacts/
#   * kotlinc/d8/aapt2 scratch -> mktemp -d under /tmp, already outside the tree
# These entries are the belt to that braces: if any of it is ever produced in-tree again, it still
# must not reach a commit, an rsync mirror, or GitHub.
**/target/
*.class
*.dex
*.apk
*.idsig

# iCloud conflict copies. They appear anywhere, are never wanted, and have broken builds here.
*[ ]2.*
*[ ]2
